-setup templates (.ps1 and .sh) detect an available provisioner (uv, winget) and search again after the install, so the clause holds for the delivered artifact class.\"\n+reversal_cost = \"Low: the alternative is a later row that widens the shipped templates; nothing here would be undone.\"\n+why_not_escalated = \"The alternative changes shipped behaviour every adopter inherits (an unprompted provisioner choice in place of their configured install) beyond the spec's named site; CLAUDE.md 'Edit conservatively'.\"\n+review = \"\"\n+\n+[decision.D-003]\n+decided = \"Sitting 001's third return (a [run] line's bare python3 is not the menu interpreter on Windows) is answered by narrowing, not by code: LLR-047 and SR-046's new clause state that bare python is exact on every platform and python3 only on POSIX, where a Windows interpreter's directory has no python3. The RESYNC entry and run_menu's docstring say a line meant for Windows declares python. TC-047 drives python3 on POSIX, the platforms the narrowed claim covers.\"\n+alternative = \"Make python3 exact on Windows with a per-launch directory holding a python3 shim.\"\n+reversal_cost = \"Medium: a later row could add a Windows mechanism and widen the claim back; the narrowed text would then be amended.\"\n+why_not_escalated = \"Every Windows mechanism found is worse than the gap: a batch-file shim (python3.cmd) re-parses %* after cmd has consumed the caret escapes, reopening the WI-227 data-argument contract for a quoted value holding a metacharacter (probed 2026-10-10 on this box: through a python3.cmd shim the trailing value a\\\"&echo INJECTED ran the echo; the direct call kept it one argument), and a copied or linked python3.exe outside the interpreter's directory loses its home (the DLL and a venv's pyvenv.cfg beside it). A python.org Windows install provides no python3 either, so a Windows-run line declaring python3 is already unusual.\"\ndiff --git a/project-trajectory/RESYNC_PACK.md b/project-trajectory/RESYNC_PACK.md\nindex 8a9e4062..1c188eaf 100644\n--- a/project-trajectory/RESYNC_PACK.md\n+++ b/project-trajectory/RESYNC_PACK.md\n@@ -7922,9 +7922,11 @@ only).\n    Windows that directory is the interpreter's own; on POSIX it is a\n    per-launch temporary directory holding `python` and `python3` shims that\n    exec the interpreter by its exact path (a system `/usr/bin/python3` may\n-   sit beside a different `/usr/bin/python`). A line that relied on a\n-   different `python` or `python3` from PATH should name that interpreter's\n-   path instead.\n+   sit beside a different `/usr/bin/python`). A Windows interpreter's\n+   directory holds no `python3`, so on Windows only a bare `python` is\n+   exact: a line meant to run there declares `python`. A line that relied on\n+   a different `python` or `python3` from PATH should name that\n+   interpreter's path instead.\n \n **Migrate (applies if you relied on committed coordinator hooks):** the kit's\n own repository no longer commits the coordinator guard's hook groups in its\ndiff --git a/project-trajectory/scripts/run_menu.py b/project-trajectory/scripts/run_menu.py\nindex 3644fe06..89819f9d 100644\n--- a/project-trajectory/scripts/run_menu.py\n+++ b/project-trajectory/scripts/run_menu.py\n@@ -227,7 +227,10 @@ def launch(command, extra):\n def _interpreter_dir(scratch):\n     \"\"\"A directory whose `python` is exactly this interpreter. On Windows that\n     is the interpreter's own directory: `sys.executable` is that directory's\n-    `python.exe` (an install's or a virtual environment's). On POSIX the\n+    `python.exe` (an install's or a virtual environment's). It holds no\n+    `python3`, so on Windows only a bare `python` is exact: a shim there\n+    would be a batch file, which re-parses its arguments (the data-argument\n+    contract above), and a copied executable loses its home. On POSIX the\n     interpreter's directory may hold another `python` (a system\n     /usr/bin/python3 beside a different /usr/bin/python, or none), so the line\n     gets `scratch`, holding shims that run `sys.executable` by its own path.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a511e73-e906-4589-9f31-ae73f8534ed7","uuid":"ab286622-c4b4-4b65-a238-c1c70b4760a6","timestamp":"2026-10-10T14:58:12.917Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"diff --git a/docs/decisions/wi-880.toml b/docs/decisions/wi-880.toml\nindex 5a2117eb..2da421c1 100644\n--- a/docs/decisions/wi-880.toml\n+++ b/docs/decisions/wi-880.toml\n@@ -1,7 +1,7 @@\n # docs/decisions/wi-880.toml — WI-880's lane: delegated decisions (coordinator).\n # Format: project-trajectory/decisions.template.toml.\n \n-high_risk = [\"D-001\"]\n+high_risk = [\"D-001\", \"D-003\"]\n \n [decision.D-001]\n decided = \"Round 2 widened the row past its four listed sites to the shipped commit-msg and pre-push hooks, which still picked Python by 'it runs', not the 3.11 floor: same class, same boundary, and the row's title covers every kit entry point. All three hooks now source one probe (hooks/kit-python.sh), so a hook without Python 3.11+ refuses commits and pushes where commit-msg and pre-push used to skip; the RESYNC entry flags that migration. Round 2 also closed round 1's POSIX residual in the [run] fix with exec shims.\"\n@@ -9,3 +9,16 @@ alternative = \"Land the four listed sites only and file the other two hooks as a\n reversal_cost = \"Medium: a shipped hook file, three hooks' behaviour and the adopters' migration note.\"\n why_not_escalated = \"Leaving them would ship LLR-021's one-probe statement untrue and leave the class open at two hooks of the same directory; coordinator-cycle §3 ends a class at its owning boundary.\"\n review = \"\"\n+\n+[decision.D-002]\n+decided = \"Sitting 001's first return (SR-032's Windows install clause binds the shipped dev-setup templates to behaviour only this repository's own Windows developer setup has) is answered by scoping: the clause, LLR-326 and TC-350 name this repository's own developer setup, and the